Echo pen input on a canvas over the video until the video catches up.

Waiting for the server's picture puts every stroke a round trip behind the pen.
The page draws the stroke itself, reproducing Xournal++'s pipeline closely
enough that the echo and the arriving picture are the same line: the same
three-point stabilizer, the same 0.3-unit admission threshold, the same
capsule-union fill. Colour, thickness and zoom come from the tool state the
server relays; with no tool state the overlay falls back to the sliders in the
settings panel.

A stroke is echoed whole until the video has caught up with its last point, and
the frames decide when that is: each carries the instant it was captured, so an
echo is retired only once a frame taken after the server could have drawn it
has been painted. When the video stalls the echo stays up, which a fixed window
into the past cannot do.

Echoing a stroke whole means drawing over ink the video already shows. The
canvas therefore sits on the capture's pixel grid, so the browser resamples it
the way it resamples the video, and is composited with mix-blend-mode: darken
over a white backdrop, so redrawing ink already on screen changes nothing.

